<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. Cough, runny nose, and hoarseness without fever, exudate, or tender nodes point to a virus. Testing for group A streptococcus is not recommended when the features are clearly viral, and antibiotics would not help. Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis seldom recurs. [S]<list><item>No strep test and no antibiotics.</item><item>Rest, fluids, warm drinks, honey, and saline rinses; acetaminophen up to 3 g a day for the throat, not ibuprofen or other NSAIDs.</item><item>Come back for a sore throat with fever and no cough, so it can be tested.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis, recovering (N00.9)</content>. Urine microscopy today shows 12 red cells per high-power field with no casts, in the range expected as the kidneys recover; blood pressure, weight, and examination normal. Dark urine within a day or two of a cold would point toward IgA nephropathy rather than a post-streptococcal relapse and would need prompt review. [S]<list><item>Six-week kidney review next week as planned.</item><item>Same-day review for dark urine, swelling, or a rise in weight.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: KDIGO 2021 Clinical Practice Guideline for the Management of Glomerular Diseases (Kidney Int 2021;100(4S):S1-S276) (2021); Clinical Practice Guideline for the Diagnosis and Management of Group A Streptococcal Pharyngitis: 2012 Update (Shulman ST, et al. Clin Infect Dis 2012;55(10):e86-102) (2012); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, chronic kidney disease and hypertension monographs (2026)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Six-week kidney review next week, as scheduled. Same-day visit for dark urine, swelling, or fever with a sore throat. Next scheduled visit 1784-10-27: six-week review: C3, kidney function, urine protein and blood, and the long-term plan. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
